Title: Does a Zotero re-extraction bump anything our watermark sees?
Created: 2026-08-21
Author: minh

--- log ---
2026-08-21T19:28Z minh created

--- body ---
## Context

Tracker: 0001. **An open question, not an asserted defect** -- it needs one
experiment before it is either.

0006 keeps a library-version watermark and applies deltas via `?since=`. That
tracks *item* changes. It is not obvious that it tracks a change in the
**extracted text of an unchanged item**, and that case is real: Zotero
re-extracts an attachment when its extractor improves, and the item's own
metadata does not change when it does.

zotero/zotero#6012 shows Zotero treating this as a first-class problem.
`Zotero.SDT.getProcessorVersion()` returns an identity like `'pdf/3/1'` and its
in-tree comment says it exists **"for consumers that store data derived from
packs, to fold into their staleness keys"**. Zotero also hashes each chunk's
source text (`sourceHash`) so an unchanged item is skipped and a changed one is
re-embedded regardless of why it changed. Both mechanisms exist because item
version alone is not sufficient.

zoteus stores data derived from Zotero's extraction. If the extractor changes
what a PDF yields, our index holds text that no longer matches what Zotero
would return -- silently, and until a manual rebuild.

## Why this is a question and not yet a finding

0006's ticket records that `fullTextSince` "already exists on both clients", so
there **may** already be a full-text version channel separate from item
versions, and the delta may already consult it. I have not read that path, and
`fulltext.sqlite` carries its own `fulltextIndexState(itemID, version)`
independent of `zotero.sqlite`'s item versions -- which suggests the two
sequences are genuinely separate.

So: establish the behaviour before designing anything. State the observation,
hold the cause.

## The experiment

1. Build an index over a small library including one PDF attachment.
2. Force Zotero to re-extract that attachment's full text without touching the
   item's metadata (delete `.zotero-ft-cache` and reopen in Zotero, or use
   Zotero's own re-index action).
3. Read `Last-Modified-Version` and `?since=` before and after. Does either
   surface the item?
4. Read `/items/<key>/fulltext` and check whether its own version moved.
5. Run zoteus's delta and check whether the passage text updated.

If the delta already picks it up, close this ticket with the evidence -- that is
a valuable negative result and the ticket has done its job. If it does not,
the fix follows Zotero's shape: fold an extraction identity into the staleness
key, and prefer a content hash over a version comparison, since a hash is
robust to *why* the text changed.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The behaviour is established by experiment and recorded, either way
- [ ] If the delta misses a re-extraction: a staleness key that catches it,
      with a test that re-extracts and asserts the passage text updated
